  {
    "id": "CRIT-LUMEN-177",
    "criterion": "FIXED 2026-08-23. WHEN SessionRepository::upsert_session persists a session's row plus its token_usage and tool_calls sub-records, THE SYSTEM SHALL commit all of them as one atomic transaction (via Connection::unchecked_transaction), NOT as separate autocommit statements -- a crash or error partway through must never leave the session row updated while its sub-records are only partially deleted/inserted.",
    "is_error_case": true,
    "judgment_call": true,
    "reasoning": "Full-codebase performance/correctness review finding (2026-08-23): upsert_session performed 1 (session upsert) + 1 (select-back) + up to N (token_usage delete+insert) + up to M (tool_calls delete+insert) separate statements under SQLite's default autocommit, each its own commit -- a session with many tool calls did dozens of independent commits instead of one, and a crash or error mid-sequence left the session row updated with sub-records in an inconsistent partial state. Fixed by wrapping the whole sequence in one transaction. unchecked_transaction (rather than requiring Connection::transaction's &mut Connection) was a judgment call: SessionRepository, like every repository in this crate, is constructed from a shared &Connection so callers can freely construct multiple repositories against the same connection in the same scope -- switching to &mut Connection would be a larger, cross-cutting signature change to every repository constructor for a fix scoped to this one method. unchecked_transaction is documented by rusqlite specifically for this shared-reference scenario; SQLite itself has no issue beginning a transaction without exclusive Rust-level mutable access. Regression: all 35 existing lumen-store tests (including the multithreaded concurrency test) continue to pass unchanged, confirming no behavioral regression; a dedicated crash-mid-transaction fault-injection test was considered but not added -- SQLite's transaction atomicity is a foundational, independently-tested guarantee, not custom logic this codebase is re-implementing, so the existing round-trip tests are the right level of coverage here."
  }
],
